    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Do not accept this offer as-is.** \n\nYou are in an ideal position: you were passively recruited, you are happy at Meta, and you have strong compensation at IC6. Google\u2019s initial offer is a **lukewarm lateral move** that asks you to take an immediate $25,000 cut in base salary while relying heavily on a front-loaded equity structure that drops significantly in later years.\n\n**Recommendation:** **Negotiate aggressively.** Since you are happy to stay at Meta, you hold all the leverage. If Google will not meet your target, **walk away**.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown & The \"Hidden\" Equity Cliff\n\nAt first glance, an $800k grant looks large, but how Google structures equity matters:\n\n#### 1. Immediate Base Salary Cut\n* **Meta Base:** $240,000\n* **Google Base:** $215,000 (A **-$25,000/yr** pay cut)\n* *Context:* The job listing range is $183,000\u2013$271,000, meaning $215,000 is on the lower-to-middle end for an L6 PM in the Bay Area.\n\n#### 2. Google's Front-Loaded Vesting Schedule\nGoogle new-hire GSU grants follow a front-loaded schedule: **38% in Year 1, 32% in Year 2, 20% in Year 3, and 10% in Year 4** (or 33/33/22/12 depending on the specific contract).\n\n| Year | Base | Target Bonus (20%) | RSUs (38/32/20/10) | Total Comp (TC) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $215,000 | $43,000 | $304,000 | **$562,000** |\n| **Year 2** | $215,000 | $43,000 | $256,000 | **$514,000** |\n| **Year 3** | $215,000 | $43,000 | $160,000 | **$418,000** *(Cliff)* |\n| **Year 4** | $215,000 | $43,000 | $80,000 | **$338,000** *(Cliff)* |\n| **4-Yr Average** | $215,000 | $43,000 | $200,000 | **$458,000/yr** |\n\n* **Year 1:** Looks higher than Meta ($562k vs. $526k) solely because of the 38% front-load.\n* **Years 3 & 4:** You face a steep drop-off. Even with annual refresher grants (which typically vest 25% evenly over 4 years), refreshes are discretionary and tied to performance ratings. It is difficult for refreshers alone to completely bridge a $140,000\u2013$220,000 drop in initial grant value without top-tier ratings.\n* **4-Year Annualized Average:** At **$458k/year**, this offer is roughly **$68,000/year less** than your current run-rate at Meta.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nTo make leaving a secure, high-performing IC6 role at Meta worthwhile, aim for a package that protects your base salary, provides a sign-on buffer, and increases initial equity:\n\n1. **Base Salary:** **$245,000 \u2013 $250,000**\n   * *Justification:* You cannot take a base salary reduction from your current $240,000. $245k\u2013$250k is well within their posted $183k\u2013$271k band.\n2. **Equity (GSUs):** **$950,000 \u2013 $1,050,000 over 4 years**\n   * *Justification:* At $1M with a 38/32/20/10 schedule, Year 1 is $380k and Year 2 is $320k, keeping your 4-year annualized average equity at $250k/year (matching your current Meta run-rate).\n3. **Sign-on Bonus:** **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n   * *Justification:* Offsets the forfeiture of your upcoming Meta bonus/RSU cycle ($200k unvested in the next 12 months) and cushions the transition risk.\n\n---\n\n### How to Communicate the Negotiation\n\nReach out to your recruiter via phone or email with a clear, polite, and confident tone:\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team and I'm excited about the scope of the L6 PM role in SF.*\n> \n> *However, as I mentioned early on, I wasn't actively looking and I'm very well-compensated and happy in my current IC6 role at Meta, where my total comp run-rate is around $526k with a $240k base.*\n> \n> *Looking at this offer, the $215k base is an immediate $25k pay cut, and because of Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule (38/32/20/10), the 4-year annualized average of the package is a step backward from where I am today without guaranteed refreshes.*\n> \n> *For me to make the move, we would need to get the package to:*\n> * * **Base Salary:** $245,000 (to match/exceed my current base within your band)*\n> * * **Equity:** $1,000,000 over 4 years (to ensure the annualized run-rate stays competitive through Years 3 and 4)*\n> * * **Sign-on Bonus:** $75,000 (to offset unvested equity and upcoming bonus cycle)*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Walk-Away Threshold\n\n* **Walk Away If:** Google refuses to increase base pay above your current $240k, offers no sign-on bonus, and leaves equity around $800k. \n* **Consider Accepting If:** They bring base to **$235k\u2013$245k**, increase equity to **$900k+**, and provide a **$50k+ sign-on bonus**.",
